Found in the k42 re-review, pre-existing (not caused by k42): the path cleaning in internal/config/permission.go turns a hand-written relative rule into a wildcard. Write(./*) and Write(x/../*) both allow Write on /etc/passwd in allowlist mode. k42 fixed the substituted case (placeholders are now always absolute), but a user-written relative pattern still widens. Fix: reject (or refuse to load) a path rule that is relative or that cleans to a bare * / root, and decide what a relative rule should mean, if anything. Regression test in internal/config: Write(./*), Write(x/../*), Write(*) on an outside path. Lane: unreagent-worker. Size S/M.

Worker done (worktree-agent-a37f74fe4818f37c6, uncommitted, based on 2e72af4): for Read/Write/Edit/MultiEdit/NotebookEdit a rule whose path is not absolute or is only a root (./*, x/../*, *, Source/*, C:*, empty, /*, C:\*, placeholders climbing to a root) is a load error naming permissions.allow|deny[i] (checked only while permissions.enabled); bare tool name = every path. At match time an allow rule with such a path never grants (covers MCP tools, Glob/Grep, tool-name globs); deny rules match as written. No documented meaning for relative rules found. Tests TestPathRulesMustBeAbsolute, TestPathAllowRulesDoNotWiden, red -> green, each fix pinned by a revert.

