Nits:
1. permission.go:279-314 (checkRules + pathPatternProblem root case, line 310). Deny rules that name a root are rejected, and the error at :290 says "write the bare tool name". That is only equivalent for /* on POSIX. On a multi-drive Windows host, deny Write(C:\*) means "never write to C:". The bare Write blocks D:, E: and UNC paths as well. No other spelling is left (C:/*, C:\.\* and C:\..\* are all rejected). A root-anchored deny rule cannot widen anything. Suggest applying the root check to allow rules only, and keeping "not absolute" for both lists.
2. Behaviour change that neither the card nor k111 mentions: Grep(*), Glob(*) and mcp__x__*(*) no longer grant a call that carries a path field. HEAD 2e72af4 granted them, as verified. This fails closed, but it is silent, because these are not load-checked. Add it to k111.
3. The root check does not know the Windows namespace prefixes. Write(\\?\C:\*) and Write(\\.\C:\*) load, while Write(C:\*) is rejected; \\.\* loads on Windows. The rule is only literal, since it never matches C:\ values, but it is a whole-drive rule in another spelling. Belongs with k109.
4. The Windows-only branch prefix == "//" (permission.go:310, the \\* root) is not pinned: removing it leaves every test green, because on Linux \\* cleans to /*. My probe shows the logic is correct with windows=true. Add a pathPatternProblem(..., true) table.
5. On Windows a drive-less rooted deny rule (README's Write(/etc/*)) is accepted but never meets C:\ paths. That is the same "inert deny" argument that justifies rejecting relative deny rules. At least document it (k111 item 1 already plans that).

